@startuml allocation_cost_factor_classes
title Cost factor in target scoring (Firestore-tunable)
skinparam classAttributeIconSize 0
skinparam packageStyle rectangle

package "serverV2/config" {
  class AllocationWeights {
    speed_weight
    cuda_weight
    os_weight
    cost_weight (new, default 0)
  }
}

package "serverV2/allocation analyzers" {
  class CompositeScorer <<module>> {
    REF_SECONDS = 300
    REF_COST = 0.10 (new)
    +score_target(price_per_hour) (new param)
    +chunk_cost_for() (already exists)
  }
}

package "serverV2/allocation strategies" {
  class AllocationPlanner {
    +scores each eligible target
    passes price = target.price_per_hour
  }
}

AllocationPlanner --> CompositeScorer : score_target(+price)
CompositeScorer --> AllocationWeights : reads cost_weight

note bottom of CompositeScorer
  base = speed*speed_factor + cuda*cuda_factor
       + os*os_factor + cost*cost_factor   (cost term = new)
  --
  speed_factor = REF_SECONDS / chunk_seconds
  cost_factor  = REF_COST / chunk_cost
  chunk_cost   = chunk_seconds / 3600 * price_per_hour
  --
  rewards LOW cost-to-render-the-chunk:
  4090 (slow, cheap/frame) -> high cost_factor
  H200 (fast, pricey/frame) -> low cost_factor
end note

note bottom of AllocationWeights
  cost_weight default 0 = today's cost-blind behaviour.
  _weights() parses it with a back-compat default so old
  Firestore docs keep working.  Raise it (start ~0.3-0.5)
  via the config page to pull the mix toward cheaper cards.
end note

note bottom of AllocationPlanner
  Two call sites (lines 209, 312).  price_per_hour is already
  on each FleetCapability, so only the arg is added.
  community stays community-first; if its price is ever set
  near 0 we cap cost_factor (flagged, not built).
end note
@enduml
